## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical streaming analytics under fixed memory, focusing on real-time percentile estimation and how rebinding affects accuracy and cost.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms, fixed memory, boundary recomputation, percentile estimation, amortized analysis.
- Trade-offs between K, R, and accuracy; cache-friendly bucket access.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Histogram85 {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M; this.K = K;
    this.bounds = Array.from({length: K}, (_,i)=> Math.floor((i+1)*M/K));
    this.counts = new Array(K).fill(0);
  }
  update(x){
    let bucket = Math.min(this.K-1, Math.floor(x/ (this.M/this.K)));
    this.counts[bucket]++;
  }
  total(){ return this.counts.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0); }
  percentile(p){ // p in [0,1]
    const total = this.total();
    const target = p * total;
    let acc = 0;
    for(let i=this.K-1; i>=0; i--){
      acc += this.counts[i];
      if(acc >= target) return this.bounds[i-1] !== undefined ? this.bounds[i-1] : this.M;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose K and R for skewed data?
- How does this scale with M and batch size B?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming distinct counting under memory constraints, a common real-world need in chat pipelines. Introduces HyperLogLog beyond basic frequency sketches and checks ability to reason about merging across shards.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for cardinality estimation
- Memory-constant sketch with m registers and 5–6 bits per register
- Per-element update and merge operations (max per register)
- Relative error ~1.04/√m; small-bias corrections for tiny cardinalities
- Trade-offs: m vs accuracy vs memory; cross-shard merging

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateHLL(M, xHash64) {
  const p = M.length ? Math.log2(M.length) : 0;
  const i = xHash64 >>> (64 - p);
  const w = (xHash64 << p) >>> 0;
  const v = 1 + Math.clz32(~w); // rough trailing zeros proxy
  M[i] = Math.max(M[i], v);
}

function estimateCardinality(M) {
  const m = M.length;
  const sum = M.reduce((acc, x) => acc + Math.pow(2, -x), 0);
  const alpha = (m === 16) ? 0.673 : (m === 32) ? 0.697 : (m === 64) ? 0.709 : 0.721;
  return (alpha * m * m) / sum;
}
```

